src/henchman/cli/textual_mixins.py\n## Date: 2025-03-17T04:00:00Z\n## Bug 
Summary\nThe `_textual_confirm` method uses `id(future)` as a dictionary key, 
which can lead to ID collisions when futures are garbage collected and memory 
addresses are reused, causing wrong future to be accessed or modified.\n\n## 
Lens\n**Phase 1: The Intent vs. Reality Check** - This is a logic gap where the 
code assumes `id()` provides a unique identifier that persists over time, but 
Python's `id()` only guarantees uniqueness among simultaneously existing 
objects, not over time.\n\n## Expected vs. Observed\n| Expected Behavior | 
Observed Risk |\n|---|---|\n| `id(future)` should provide a unique identifier 
that can safely be used as a dictionary key for the lifetime of the confirmation
process | `id()` returns memory address which can be reused after object is 
garbage collected, leading to potential key collisions |\n| Each confirmation 
future should have a truly unique ID that won't conflict with other futures | If
Future A is garbage collected and Future B is allocated at same memory address, 
they get same `id()`, causing callbacks for Future A to affect Future B |\n\n## 
Root Cause\nIn the `_textual_confirm` method:\n```python\nreq_id = 
id(future)\nself._confirm_futures[req_id] = future  # type: 
ignore[attr-defined]\n\ndef _callback(result: object | None) -> None:\n    if 
not future.done():\n        future.set_result(bool(result))\n    
self._confirm_futures.pop(req_id, None)  # type: 
ignore[attr-defined]\n```\n\nThe bug is that `id(future)` is not a reliable 
unique identifier over time. Python's `id()` function returns the memory address
of an object, which can be reused after the object is garbage collected. In a 
long-running application with many confirmation dialogs:\n1. Future A is 
created, gets ID 1000 (memory address)\n2. Future A completes, callback removes 
it from dict, future is garbage collected\n3. Future B is created, gets 
allocated at same memory address, ID 1000\n4. Late callback for Future A (race 
condition) tries to pop ID 1000, affecting Future B\n5. Or 
`_cleanup_confirm_futures` might cancel Future B thinking it's Future A\n\n## 
Fix Plan\nReplace `id(future)` with a proper unique ID generator. Options:\n1. 
Use `uuid.uuid4()` for truly unique IDs\n2. Use an incrementing counter 
(`self._confirm_counter` already exists but is unused)\n3. Use `future` object 
itself as key (requires weakref to avoid memory leaks)\n\nSimplest fix: Use the 
existing `self._confirm_counter` that's already initialized but unused.\n\n## 
Status: IN PROGRESS"})

